"An insertion process and a parity based equidistribution (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.14508)\nciteturn0academia11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Title: An insertion process and a parity based equidistribution ... A conjecture by Deutsch, Kitaev, and Remmel states that the triples of permutation statistics $(S_{10}, S_{12}, S_{17})$ and $(S_{12}, S_{10} ,S_{17})$ are equidistributed over the symmetric group $\\mathfrak{S}_n$. Here, $S_{10}$ enumerates descents with odd descent tops, $S_{12}$ enumerates odd-odd adjacent pairs, and $S_{17}$ records the largest integer $i$ such that $1, 2, \\dots, i$ appear in left-to-right order.\nTitle: An insertion process and a parity based equidistribution\nAuthors: Umesh Shankar\nDate: Sun Mar 15 17:40:59 2026\n\nA conjecture by Deutsch, Kitaev, and Remmel states that the triples of permutation statistics $(S_{10}, S_{12}, S_{17})$ and $(S_{12}, S_{10} ,S_{17})$ are equidistributed over the symmetric group $\\mathfrak{S}_n$. Here, $S_{10}$ enumerates descents with odd descent tops, $S_{12}$ enumerates odd-odd adjacent pairs, and $S_{17}$ records the largest integer $i$ such that $1, 2, \\dots, i$ appear in left-to-right order. In this note, we resolve this conjecture affirmatively by providing a bijective proof. We introduce an insertion process that constructs a recursive involution on $\\mathfrak{S}_n$ that swaps $S_{10}$ and $S_{12}$ while keeping $S_{17}$ unchanged.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAné–Eulenstein conjecture on maximal groves · ProbXiv (https://probxiv.com/problems/ane-eulenstein-conjecture-on-maximal-groves)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Ané–Eulenstein conjecture on maximal groves ... A grove is maximal in $\\mathcal{S}$S if it is not properly contained in another grove in $\\mathcal{S}$S. ...   * Mareike Fischer, “Mathematical aspects of phylogenetic groves”, arXiv:1104.2562 (2011).\n\n# Ané–Eulenstein conjecture on maximal groves\n\nmath.CO · posed by 2011 · open\n\n## Statement\n\nLet $\\mathcal{S}$S be a set of taxon sets, and let a grove be a collection of taxon sets with the potential to construct informative supertrees. A grove is maximal in $\\mathcal{S}$S if it is not properly contained in another grove in $\\mathcal{S}$S. The following properties are equivalent: Ané–Eulenstein conjecture.\n\n  1. For any set $\\mathcal{S}$S of taxon sets, the set of maximal groves in $\\mathcal{S}$S is a partition of $\\mathcal{S}$S.\n  2. If two groves intersect, their union is a grove.\n  3. Two maximal groves do not intersect.\n\nThe conjecture concerns when overlap among taxon sets guarantees that collections of phylogenies can be combined informatively; the source attributes it to Ané et al. and presents it as a criterion relevant to identifying useful groves in databases. The paper later notes that the analogous property fails for informative groves in general.\n\n## Record\n\nSource\n    \n\n  * Mareike Fischer, “Mathematical aspects of phylogenetic groves”, arXiv:1104.2562 (2011).\n  * MathDB\n\nAdded\n    Oct 8, 2026\n\n## Comments\n\nNothing has been published against this problem yet, and nothing has been said about it.\n\nSign in with an institutional address to take part in the discussion. Reading every thread stays open to everyone.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEquidistribution of (X,Y)-descents, (X,Y)-adjacent pairs, and (X,Y)-place-value pairs on permutations (https://arxiv.org/abs/0903.2555)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.7 years ago; Title: Equidistribution of (X,Y)-descents, (X,Y)-adjacent pairs, and (X,Y)-place-value pairs on permutations\nAuthors: Emeric Deutsch, Sergey Kitaev, Jeffrey Remmel ... An $(X,Y)$-descent in a permutation is a pair of adjacent elements such that the first element is from $X$, the second element is from $Y$, and the first element is greater than the second one. ... We also conjecture existence of a bijection between two objects in question preserving a certain statistic.\nTitle: Equidistribution of (X,Y)-descents, (X,Y)-adjacent pairs, and (X,Y)-place-value pairs on permutations\nAuthors: Emeric Deutsch, Sergey Kitaev, Jeffrey Remmel\nDate: Sat Mar 14 16:15:29 2009\n\nAn $(X,Y)$-descent in a permutation is a pair of adjacent elements such that the first element is from $X$, the second element is from $Y$, and the first element is greater than the second one. An $(X,Y)$-adjacency in a permutation is a pair of adjacent elements such that the first one is from $X$ and the second one is from $Y$. An $(X,Y)$-place-value pair in a permutation is an element $y$ in position $x$, such that $y$ is in $Y$ and $x$ is in $X$. It turns out, that for certain choices of $X$ and $Y$ some of the three statistics above become equidistributed. Moreover, it is easy to derive the distribution formula for $(X,Y)$-place-value pairs thus providing distribution for other statistics under consideration too. This generalizes some results in the literature. As a result of our considerations, we get combinatorial proofs of several remarkable identities. We also conjecture existence of a bijection between two objects in question preserving a certain statistic.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEquidistribution of Descents, Adjacent Pairs, and Place-Value Pairs on Permutations (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/24164565_Equidistribution_of_Descents_Adjacent_Pairs_and_Place-Value_Pairs_on_Permutations)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; Recall that the statistic S 10 is the number of odd descent-tops, and S 12 is the number ... Our ﬁrst conjecture is the following joint equidistribution: ... Notice, that Conjecture 11 suggests existence of an involution turning S 10 to S 12 and ... Here is how a proof of Conjecture 12 could be arranged for even n assuming the rest 